  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 AM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Another hot and humid day today, with a chance of \nan isolated shower/storm this afternoon. \n\nA very similar weather pattern continues for central NC today. With \nsurface high pressure located off the southeast coast and a deep \nridge centered to our west, high temperatures this afternoon will \nremain in the low 90s north to the mid 90s in the south. Dewpoint \ntemperatures this afternoon look to mix out to the upper 60s to \naround 70s. This should help keep heat indices below Heat Advisory \ncriteria, with heat indices looking to peak in the 90s to around \n102. Although a Heat Advisory has not been issued, this level of \nheat could still pose risks for those without adequate cooling and \nhydration today, especially those sensitive to high heat.\n\nRain chances this afternoon will come from a mid/upper level \ndisturbance that looks to move through central NC this afternoon \ninto this evening. Although CAMs have been trending down with the \namount of convection expected, still think isolated showers and \nthunderstorms will be possible this afternoon into the evening \nmainly in the Western Piedmont. The amount of convection expected \nshould be better understood as we see how upstream convection \nbehaves this morning and if it can be sustained as it moves across \nthe mountains. Looking locally, CAPE values this afternoon look to \nmaximize around 1500-2000 J/kg, with around 20 kts of 0-6km shear. \nAs a result, a few storms could produce strong to damaging wind \ngusts if they are able to prevail into central NC this \nafternoon/evening. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The heat will continue through late week, with a \nchance for some afternoon convection. \n\nCentral NC will be on the nrn periphery of the strengthening sub-\ntropical ridge, with a series of s/w disturbances tracking across \nthe area within the nwly flow aloft. At the surface, a trough will \nsit over the region Thu and Fri, a low developing within it as a \nbackdoor front drops swd across the mid-Atlantic on Fri. There is \nstill a good bit of uncertainty wrt convection both Thu and Fri, but \nexpect a chance for aft/eve showers/storms each day, but confidence \nis not high on occurrence or coverage. As for temperatures, highs \nshould range from low 90s north to upper 90s/near 100 degrees south. \nAfternoon RH values in the upr 60s to mid 70s will result in heat \nindex values ranging from around 100 north to 105-110 south, highest \nFri.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Expect temperatures to back of a bit over the \nweekend, while chances for diurnal convection continue.\n\nThe mid-level anti-cyclone will strengthen and shift gradually wnwwd \nthrough the weekend and into early next week, with the longwave \ntrough becoming more amplified along the East Coast early-mid week. \nShortwave disturbances riding the ridge may move across the area \nover the weekend and early next week. At the surface, model guidance \nvaries wrt the track and strength of the high, and whether the front \nwill make it across central NC over the weekend. There should at \nleast be some nely/ely component of the wind into portions of \ncentral NC over the weekend as the high slides sewd across the mid-\nAtlantic and off the East Coast. With the backdoor front potential \nand some nely/ely flow at the surface, Sat and Sun should be less \nhot than Thu and Fri, but still above normal.",
